\chapter*{How to Use This Pamphlet}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{How to Use This Pamphlet}
An intermediate representation becomes useful when another component depends on it.
This pamphlet develops one small enough to inspect, then uses it to compile and execute programs.
The implementation is C11 in \code{example-code/pebbleir/}; its README gives build commands, and its test program supplies the executable checks discussed here.
The source language, IR interpreter, optimizer, text format, and virtual-machine lowering form one working path.
Branches and loops are exercised through the IR builder even though the expression front end has no statement syntax.

Read the first four chapters before extending the instruction set.
Read the verifier and mutation chapters together before adding an optimization.
Use the final chapter's workflow whenever a new client exposes an inconvenient assumption.
Every chapter ends with exercises; worked answers are kept in \code{book/SOLUTIONS.md}.
Pseudocode describes either the implemented mechanism or an explicitly identified extension.
The implementation has fixed capacities, two-input phis, one byte-valued argument, and no memory operations.
These choices keep the correctness obligations visible; the chapters explain where a larger design must change them.

This companion shares the main book's typography and bibliography but has its own chapter numbers and index.
Compile \code{book/pamphlet.ltx} through the supplied driver from the repository root.
The companion PDF is a separate document, so it can be read beside the book or distributed with it.
